129","location":"Assembly","sequence":24,"significance":"major","source":null,"status":null,"text":"Senate amendments concurred in. To Engrossing and Enrolling. (Ayes 77. Noes 0. Page 6566.).","time":null,"votes":[]},{"classificationRule":"ca-enrolled","code":null,"date":"2026-09-03","eventType":"enrolled","historyId":"119124131","location":"Governor","sequence":25,"significance":"major","source":null,"status":null,"text":"Enrolled and presented to the Governor at 4 p.m.","time":null,"votes":[]},{"classificationRule":"ca-approved","code":null,"date":"2026-09-30","eventType":"approved_by_governor","historyId":"119124133","location":"Governor","sequence":26,"significance":"major","source":null,"status":null,"text":"Approved by the Governor.","time":null,"votes":[]},{"classificationRule":"ca-chaptered","code":null,"date":"2026-09-30","eventType":"chaptered","historyId":"119124135","location":"Secretary of State","sequence":27,"significance":"major","source":null,"status":null,"text":"Chaptered by Secretary of State - Chapter 880, Statutes of 2026.","time":null,"votes":[]}],"affectedStatutes":[{"action":"IS_AMENDED","code":"ELEC","text":"Section 6901.5 of the Elections Code is amended to read:"},{"action":"IS_AMENDED","code":"ELEC","text":"Section 8304 of the Elections Code is amended to read:"},{"action":"IS_AMENDED","code":"ELEC","text":"Section 8650 of the Elections Code is amended to read:"}],"approvedDate":"2026-09-30","chapter":{"citation":"Chapter 880, Statutes of 2026","number":"880","type":"CHP","year":"2026"},"chapteredDate":"2026-09-30","committees":[],"conditions":[],"contentHash":"b202aa4e75880502d590cef21671892d5864951094b5e598b3a24c9a037b08e6","cosponsorCount":null,"digest":"The Twenty-Second Amendment to the United States Constitution prohibits the election of any person to the office of the President more than twice. Under existing state law, each qualified political party is required to notify the Secretary of State of the names of the party’s nominees for President and Vice President on or before the 75th day before a presidential general election. Existing law requires the Secretary of State to cause the names of each party’s nominees for President and Vice President to be placed upon the ballot for the election.\n\nThis bill would require a representative of each qualified political party, and a representative of each independent presidential candidate or write-in presidential candidate, to certify to the Secretary of State, under penalty of perjury, that their nominees for President and Vice President are both qualified to be elected President under the Twenty-Second Amendment. If that certification is not made by the 75th day before the general election for the candidate of each qualified political party and an independent candidate, or by the 14th day before the general election for a write-in candidate, the bill would prohibit placing the names of those nominees for President and Vice President upon the ballot for the election. By expanding the crime of perjury, the bill would impose a state-mandated local program.\n\nThe California Constitution requires the state to reimburse local agencies and school districts for certain costs mandated by the state. Statutory provisions establish procedures for making that reimbursement.\n\nThis bill would provide that no reimbursement is required by this act for a specified reason.","digestDate":null,"digestVersion":null,"effective":{"basis":"not_verified","date":null,"note":"No effective date is recorded in the official bill data and DemocracyHQ has not verified one. A non-urgency statute enacted in a regular session generally takes effect on January 1 after a 90-day period (Cal. Const. art. IV, § 8(c)), but a bill may state other operative dates."},"expiryClauses":[],"firstObservedAt":"2026-11-20T01:12:59Z","id":"ca-20252026-ab1539","identifier":"AB 1539","indicators":{"Appropriation":"NO","BudgetBill":"NO","Election":"NO","FiscalCommittee":"YES","ImmediateEffect":"N